window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-06-1", "source": "2022年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose you are going to write a proposal to your school library for improving its service. You are to write about its current problems and possible solutions to these problems. You will have 30 minutes to write the proposal. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "I am a sophomore who spends most of my free time studying in our school library. As a frequent user, I am\nwriting to propose several improvements that I believe would enhance its service.\nTwo problems stand out. First, the library closes at nine every evening, which is too early for students who\nprepare for exams late at night; I suggest extending the opening hours to eleven during examination weeks.\nSecond, the reading area is often noisy because there is no separated quiet zone, so it is hard to concentrate. It\nwould be advisable to set aside a silent room and remind readers to keep their voices down.\nI believe these changes are simple yet effective, and they would make the library far more welcoming to every\nstudent. Thank you very much for considering my suggestions, and I sincerely hope they can be adopted soon."}, "translation": {"prompt": "从前有个农夫嫌他种的禾苗长得慢，就到地里把禾苗一株株地拔高了一点。回家后他对家人说：\"今天可真把我累坏了！但我总算让禾苗一下子长高了。\"他儿子到地里去一看，禾苗都已死光了。 现在有些家长急于让孩子成功，往往步那个农夫的后尘，搞得孩子苦不堪言，却不见孩子学业长进。这样的家长是否该对这个问题有所醒悟，让孩子自然成长呢？", "sample": "Once upon a time, a farmer, thinking that the rice seedlings he had planted were growing too slowly, went into the field and pulled them up one by one to make each a little taller. After returning home, he told his family, \"I'm really worn out today, but I have finally made the rice seedlings grow taller all at once!\" When his son went to the field, he found that all the rice seedlings had already withered. Nowadays, some parents, eager to see their children succeed, often follow in the farmer's footsteps, causing their children great suffering, yet seeing no progress in their studies. Shouldn't such parents wake up to this problem and let their children grow naturally?"}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2022-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "You might think of your teeth as tools, like built-in knives and forks, but if they are mere tools, why do they feel pain and wouldn't it be better if they could just  __26__  under any condition? In spite of our  __27__  discomfort, it turns out there's a good reason our teeth are so sensitive. Tooth pain is a  __28__  mechanism that ensures when a tooth is being damaged we'll notice and do something about it.\nIf we eat something too hot or too cold, or if the tooth is worn down enough where the tissue  __29__  is exposed, all of those things cause pain, and then the pain causes the person not to use that tooth to try to protect it a little bit more. So it's really a protective mechanism more than anything else. If teeth didn't feel pain, we might  __30__  to use them in situations that damage them, and for humans, damaging  __31__  teeth is a problem because, unlike crocodiles, we can't  __32__  them.\nTeeth have three layers, only one of which—the innermost layer of the tooth—can hurt, as that layer of the tooth  __33__  both blood vessels and nerves. Pain is the only feeling to which the nerves in that layer respond. Whereas people with tooth sensitivity may complain, for example, of tooth pain  __34__  by heat or cold, the nerves in the inner layer don't sense temperature. Rather, they feel pain, which may be  __35__  with, say, drinking something very cold.", "matchIntro": "How to determine if a company is a good fit for you", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "On paper, the job seemed perfect for me: The position was completely in line with my degree, the duties and responsibilities were compatible with my interests, and the office maintained a well-stocked kitchen that would satisfy my every snack desire."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sounds like my dream job, right? There was only one small problem: I simply didn't get along with the company culture. They favored a more rigid, closed-door, corporate atmosphere, while I would have preferred something more collaborative and open. They were complete clock watchers, while I would have liked a more flexible schedule. To put it plainly, we just weren't on the same page."}, {"label": "C", "text": "When it comes to looking for a new job, you already know that a big part of the interview process involves the company evaluating whether or not you're a good fit for their open role. But, it's important to keep in mind that the employer isn't the only one who needs to identify a good match—you should be looking for that same exact thing. Company culture can have a big impact on how you feel about your work, so you want to make sure you sign an offer letter with an organization you're truly excited about."}, {"label": "D", "text": "However, figuring out what a company is like before you actually work there can be a bit of a challenge. Luckily, there are a few things you can do to determine whether or not a company is a good fit for you—before you ever sign your name on that dotted line."}, {"label": "E", "text": "First of all, know what you want. It's hard to make any decisions when you don't really know what you're looking for. So before you can determine whether you and a specific company would be compatible together, it's important to have a solid handle on what exactly you want from your employer. Many of us have an easier time identifying the things that we absolutely don't want. If those are the only things you can think of, don't worry! That's still a good place to start."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Start by writing down the things you didn't like about previous employers, as well as the parts you really valued. There's no wrong answer here—so from big things to small details, write them all down on your list. This will help you immediately identify what you're looking for in an organization, as well as the things you're trying to stay far, far away from."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Make sure to do your research. Now comes the part when you put on your detective hat and do a little digging. The Internet will be your best friend when you're trying to familiarize yourself with a company's culture before ever walking through their office doors. And where exactly should you look for these culture clues? Start with the most obvious place first: the company's website. Read through their copy and blog. Do they use formal, direct language? Or is it casual, conversational, and maybe even a little humorous? This can be a big indicator of what sort of atmosphere the company is trying to cultivate."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Next, turn your attention toward their social media outlets. Are they sharing photos of their team's Thursday afternoon barbeque or Halloween costume contest? Or are their social media accounts strictly reserved for company-related announcements and product launches?"}, {"label": "I", "text": "An industry review website like Glassdoor is another spot to check in order to find some insider information about what you can expect about a company. However, remember to take the reviews you read with a grain of salt—many of them are written by scorned(被鄙视的) employees."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Finally, you can never fail with personal connections. Send a quick message to a current or previous company employee on LinkedIn or by email and ask if they would be willing to have a quick conversation with you about the organization in general. If you get a yes to your request for a chat, you'll be armed with some pretty powerful and helpful information heading into your interview!"}, {"label": "K", "text": "Learn more by asking questions. You know that part at the end of a job interview when the hiring manager asks if you have any questions, and you just stare across the table blankly with your mouth hanging open? That's the perfect opportunity to speak up and get your burning company culture questions answered! So yes, you can definitely ask your interviewer about what it's like to work for that particular organization. Simple questions like, \"What three words would you use to describe the culture here?\" or \"What's your favorite part about working for this company?\" can reveal a lot about what it's really like behind closed doors."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Prioritize your values. What does my dream company culture look like? Well, I could come and go as I please, as long as I was getting the work done. My boss would genuinely listen to and value all of my ideas and suggestions. My co-workers would all be friendly with one another, without ever falling into the office gossip trap. The kitchen would have endless options of pizza and cookies. Oh, and they'd give me two months of paid vacation with a very generous salary."}, {"label": "M", "text": "What are my chances of finding all of those things with one employer? Slim to none—believe me, I've looked. This is why it's so important to know which aspects of a company's culture you value most. Is it an open communication style or a flexible schedule? Focus on the top spots on your priority list, and ensure a potential employer at least checks those boxes. Unfortunately, this is reality, you can't have everything you want but a few are certainly achievable."}, {"label": "N", "text": "When you're hunting for a new job, you already know that the employer is trying to decide whether or not you're a good fit for the position. But you should also look at the process through a similar lens. You may not be the one conducting the actual interview, but you're still trying to determine whether or not the company is a good fit for you."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Keep these tips in mind to figure out whether you and a potential employer are a perfect match or just a recipe(方案) for disaster. After all, it's a good thing to know before actually accepting an offer."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Online classes began to be popularized just a few decades ago. They are advertised as a way for adults to finish their education and students to learn the material at their own pace—it is far more compatible for people with busy schedules.\nBut after being enrolled in an online course last fall semester, I came to realize online classes were merely a means to fulfil course requirements.\nFirst of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\nAs online courses tend to have more than 100 students, most of the assignments are short and simple. They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be graded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\nPerhaps the biggest disadvantage of taking an online class is the absence of face-to-face interaction between the teacher and their students. Live sessions are infrequent and are often scheduled during the middle of the day when students have to attend other classes or work. The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times for many students as well. Most interaction with the professor has to be through email which is often impersonal. It is nearly impossible for students to build a relationship with their professor.\nThere is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study groups and form relationships with their peers.\nOnline classes also require either a computer or laptop and a reliable internet connection. Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other reasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\nOffering online classes certainly helps students who would otherwise not be able to attend classroom sessions. However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather than critical thinking. We need restructured online classes in which students can have a learning experience that will actually provide quality education.", "47": "Online classes began to be popularized just a few decades ago. They are advertised as a way for adults to finish their education and students to learn the material at their own pace—it is far more compatible for people with busy schedules.\nBut after being enrolled in an online course last fall semester, I came to realize online classes were merely a means to fulfil course requirements.\nFirst of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\nAs online courses tend to have more than 100 students, most of the assignments are short and simple. They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be graded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\nPerhaps the biggest disadvantage of taking an online class is the absence of face-to-face interaction between the teacher and their students. Live sessions are infrequent and are often scheduled during the middle of the day when students have to attend other classes or work. The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times for many students as well. Most interaction with the professor has to be through email which is often impersonal. It is nearly impossible for students to build a relationship with their professor.\nThere is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study groups and form relationships with their peers.\nOnline classes also require either a computer or laptop and a reliable internet connection. Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other reasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\nOffering online classes certainly helps students who would otherwise not be able to attend classroom sessions. However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather than critical thinking. We need restructured online classes in which students can have a learning experience that will actually provide quality education.", "48": "Online classes began to be popularized just a few decades ago. They are advertised as a way for adults to finish their education and students to learn the material at their own pace—it is far more compatible for people with busy schedules.\nBut after being enrolled in an online course last fall semester, I came to realize online classes were merely a means to fulfil course requirements.\nFirst of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\nAs online courses tend to have more than 100 students, most of the assignments are short and simple. They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be graded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\nPerhaps the biggest disadvantage of taking an online class is the absence of face-to-face interaction between the teacher and their students. Live sessions are infrequent and are often scheduled during the middle of the day when students have to attend other classes or work. The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times for many students as well. Most interaction with the professor has to be through email which is often impersonal. It is nearly impossible for students to build a relationship with their professor.\nThere is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study groups and form relationships with their peers.\nOnline classes also require either a computer or laptop and a reliable internet connection. Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other reasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\nOffering online classes certainly helps students who would otherwise not be able to attend classroom sessions. However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather than critical thinking. We need restructured online classes in which students can have a learning experience that will actually provide quality education.", "49": "Online classes began to be popularized just a few decades ago. They are advertised as a way for adults to finish their education and students to learn the material at their own pace—it is far more compatible for people with busy schedules.\nBut after being enrolled in an online course last fall semester, I came to realize online classes were merely a means to fulfil course requirements.\nFirst of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\nAs online courses tend to have more than 100 students, most of the assignments are short and simple. They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be graded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\nPerhaps the biggest disadvantage of taking an online class is the absence of face-to-face interaction between the teacher and their students. Live sessions are infrequent and are often scheduled during the middle of the day when students have to attend other classes or work. The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times for many students as well. Most interaction with the professor has to be through email which is often impersonal. It is nearly impossible for students to build a relationship with their professor.\nThere is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study groups and form relationships with their peers.\nOnline classes also require either a computer or laptop and a reliable internet connection. Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other reasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\nOffering online classes certainly helps students who would otherwise not be able to attend classroom sessions. However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather than critical thinking. We need restructured online classes in which students can have a learning experience that will actually provide quality education.", "50": "Online classes began to be popularized just a few decades ago. They are advertised as a way for adults to finish their education and students to learn the material at their own pace—it is far more compatible for people with busy schedules.\nBut after being enrolled in an online course last fall semester, I came to realize online classes were merely a means to fulfil course requirements.\nFirst of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\nAs online courses tend to have more than 100 students, most of the assignments are short and simple. They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be graded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\nPerhaps the biggest disadvantage of taking an online class is the absence of face-to-face interaction between the teacher and their students. Live sessions are infrequent and are often scheduled during the middle of the day when students have to attend other classes or work. The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times for many students as well. Most interaction with the professor has to be through email which is often impersonal. It is nearly impossible for students to build a relationship with their professor.\nThere is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study groups and form relationships with their peers.\nOnline classes also require either a computer or laptop and a reliable internet connection. Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other reasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\nOffering online classes certainly helps students who would otherwise not be able to attend classroom sessions. However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather than critical thinking. We need restructured online classes in which students can have a learning experience that will actually provide quality education.", "51": "In the age of the internet, there's no such thing as a private debate. But is that bad for science? Some scientists have had concerns. When debates in any sector move beyond the halls of universities and government agencies, there's potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion; yet, open debate can also promote communication between the scientific community and the public. Recent open debates on scientific research, health, and policy have aroused greater public attention and encouraged more diverse voices. If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—and at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\nA recent debate published in The New York Times discussed the question of how quickly medicine should be developed and produced. Issues such as safety of the product and perception of the public were examined and considered. But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine.\nThe anxiety seems misplaced. Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good because those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices. These days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\nFor many scientists, public debate is a new frontier and it may feel like a place with few restraints or rules. But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous, wherever they are held. If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed. With the validity of science coming under attack, there's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life. Let everyone see the noisy, messy deliberations that advance science and lead to decisions that benefit us all.", "52": "In the age of the internet, there's no such thing as a private debate. But is that bad for science? Some scientists have had concerns. When debates in any sector move beyond the halls of universities and government agencies, there's potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion; yet, open debate can also promote communication between the scientific community and the public. Recent open debates on scientific research, health, and policy have aroused greater public attention and encouraged more diverse voices. If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—and at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\nA recent debate published in The New York Times discussed the question of how quickly medicine should be developed and produced. Issues such as safety of the product and perception of the public were examined and considered. But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine.\nThe anxiety seems misplaced. Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good because those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices. These days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\nFor many scientists, public debate is a new frontier and it may feel like a place with few restraints or rules. But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous, wherever they are held. If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed. With the validity of science coming under attack, there's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life. Let everyone see the noisy, messy deliberations that advance science and lead to decisions that benefit us all.", "53": "In the age of the internet, there's no such thing as a private debate. But is that bad for science? Some scientists have had concerns. When debates in any sector move beyond the halls of universities and government agencies, there's potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion; yet, open debate can also promote communication between the scientific community and the public. Recent open debates on scientific research, health, and policy have aroused greater public attention and encouraged more diverse voices. If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—and at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\nA recent debate published in The New York Times discussed the question of how quickly medicine should be developed and produced. Issues such as safety of the product and perception of the public were examined and considered. But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine.\nThe anxiety seems misplaced. Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good because those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices. These days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\nFor many scientists, public debate is a new frontier and it may feel like a place with few restraints or rules. But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous, wherever they are held. If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed. With the validity of science coming under attack, there's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life. Let everyone see the noisy, messy deliberations that advance science and lead to decisions that benefit us all.", "54": "In the age of the internet, there's no such thing as a private debate. But is that bad for science? Some scientists have had concerns. When debates in any sector move beyond the halls of universities and government agencies, there's potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion; yet, open debate can also promote communication between the scientific community and the public. Recent open debates on scientific research, health, and policy have aroused greater public attention and encouraged more diverse voices. If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—and at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\nA recent debate published in The New York Times discussed the question of how quickly medicine should be developed and produced. Issues such as safety of the product and perception of the public were examined and considered. But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine.\nThe anxiety seems misplaced. Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good because those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices. These days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\nFor many scientists, public debate is a new frontier and it may feel like a place with few restraints or rules. But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous, wherever they are held. If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed. With the validity of science coming under attack, there's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life. Let everyone see the noisy, messy deliberations that advance science and lead to decisions that benefit us all.", "55": "In the age of the internet, there's no such thing as a private debate. But is that bad for science? Some scientists have had concerns. When debates in any sector move beyond the halls of universities and government agencies, there's potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion; yet, open debate can also promote communication between the scientific community and the public. Recent open debates on scientific research, health, and policy have aroused greater public attention and encouraged more diverse voices. If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—and at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\nA recent debate published in The New York Times discussed the question of how quickly medicine should be developed and produced. Issues such as safety of the product and perception of the public were examined and considered. But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine.\nThe anxiety seems misplaced. Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good because those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices. These days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\nFor many scientists, public debate is a new frontier and it may feel like a place with few restraints or rules. But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous, wherever they are held. If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed. With the validity of science coming under attack, there's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life. Let everyone see the noisy, messy deliberations that advance science and lead to decisions that benefit us all."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the new study from the news report?", "options": ["A) It studied the effects of exercise on sleep.", "B) Its participants came from various walks of life.", "C) Its findings confirmed those of previous studies.", "D) It ran for as long as some thirty years."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It ran for as long as some thirty years.\n【定位】They were followed for approximately three decades.\n他们被跟踪研究了大约三十年。\n【信号】时长事实 three decades。答案句的信号是一个明确的时长数据 followed for\napproximately three decades，three decades 即三十年；新闻里凡出现具体年限、数\n字，往往就是出题落点，听到就应锁定。\n【替换】approximately three decades ↔ as long as some thirty years\nthree decades = thirty years\nThey were followed for ↔ It ran for\n被跟踪＝研究持续\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说研究的是运动对睡眠的影响，盗用了录音 questions about sleep and exercise 里真出\n现过的 sleep、exercise 两个原词；但录音里这两项只是参与者被问到的调查内容之一，本\n研究的真正主题是含糖饮料与死亡风险，并非研究运动对睡眠的影响，听到原词就选正中圈\n套。\n借用原文：questions about sleep and exercise and health\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说参与者来自各行各业（various walks of life），但录音明说 Participants worked in\nthe health profession.，即都是医疗卫生行业的人；B 与原文事实相反，是对参与者构成的\n曲解。\n借用原文：Participants worked in the health profession.\nC) 未提及\nC 说本研究证实了以往研究的结论，但录音通篇没提与任何 previous studies 的比较，反而\n在结尾强调 it did not prove that drinks with added sugar cause early death——只是\n关联、未下定论；C 属录音根本没说的内容。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is the scientists' recommendation?", "options": ["A) Eating more vegetables instead of meats.", "B) Drinking water instead of beverages with added sugar.", "C) Consuming more energy drinks and sports drinks.", "D) Forming the habit of exercising regularly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Drinking water instead of beverages with added sugar.\n【定位】They argue we should replace them with other drinks, with water being the best choice.\n他们主张我们应当用其他饮品来替代含糖饮料，其中水是最佳选择。\n【信号】建议句 should + 最佳选择。答案句同时出现两个强信号：建议词 we should replace\nthem 和择优表达 water being the best choice；should 表建议、the best choice 直接\n点名最优做法，是这句成为答案句的标志。\n【替换】replace them with other drinks, with water being the best choice ↔\nDrinking water instead of beverages with added sugar\n用水替换＝喝水代替\nthem ↔ beverages with added sugar\n代词 them 还原即含糖饮料\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说多吃蔬菜代替肉，盗用了录音 not eating enough vegetables 与 consuming too\nmuch meat 里出现过的 vegetables、meat；但录音里这两项是研究者考虑过的其他影响\n健康的因素，不是科学家给出的建议，把干扰因素当成建议正是陷阱。\n借用原文：lack of exercise and not eating enough vegetables\nC) 与原文相反\nC 让人多喝能量饮料和运动饮料，盗用录音 soda, energy drinks and sports drinks 里的\n原词；但录音明确说这些正是含糖饮料、应当限制，C 把该少喝的说成多喝，与原文意思相\n反。\n借用原文：These beverages included soda, energy drinks and sports drinks.\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说养成规律运动的习惯，盗用录音 lack of exercise 里的 exercise；但运动同样只是研究\n者排查的其他健康因素之一，录音从未把规律运动作为科学家的建议提出，D 答非所问。\n借用原文：These factors included lack of exercise\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 German police appealed Friday for information about the possible owners of two 17th-century\noil paintings.\n德国警方周五呼吁民众提供有关两幅17世纪油画可能所有者的信息。\n2 Police said a 64-year-old man found the paintings in a garbage pile at a highway rest stop last month.\nHe later handed them in to the Cologne police.\n警方表示,一名64岁男子上个月在高速公路休息站的垃圾堆中发现了这些画作。他后来将它们交给了科隆\n警方。\n3 An initial assessment by an art expert concluded the two framed paintings were originals, police said.\n警方称,一位艺术专家的初步评估认定这两幅带框画作是原作。\n4 One is a landscape painted by the Italian artist Pietro Bellotti dating to 1665. The other is a painting\nof a boy by the 17th-century Dutch artist Samuel van Hoogstraten, date unknown. Their combined worth\nis estimated to be around one million euros.\n其中一幅是意大利艺术家Pietro Bellotti于1665年创作的风景画。另一幅是17世纪荷兰艺术家Samuel\nvan Hoogstraten创作的男孩肖像画,创作日期不详。两幅画的总价值估计约为一百万欧元。\n5 Authorities have not yet confirmed what will happen if the rightful owner is not found. Nevertheless,\nit is speculated that they could either be handed over to the National Art Museum of Cologne or sold to\nthe public by the local government.\n当局尚未确认如果找不到合法所有者将如何处理。不过,有人推测这些画作可能会被移交给科隆国家美术\n馆,或由地方政府向公众出售。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What did German police say about a 64-year-old man?", "options": ["A) He asked them about his lost paintings.", "B) He knew the owner of two missing paintings.", "C) He left his paintings at a highway rest stop.", "D) He found two 17th-century oil paintings."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He found two 17th-century oil paintings.\n【定位】Police said a 64-year-old man found the paintings in a garbage pile at a highway rest stop\nlast month.\n警方称，上个月一名 64 岁的男子在一处高速公路服务区的垃圾堆里发现了这些画。\n【信号】对象出场即答案句。录音念到 a 64-year-old man 这个题干点名的对象时，紧跟其后的动\n词 found the paintings 就是答案落点——细节题的答案句信号，就是题干那个专有对象一\n出现。\n【替换】found the paintings ↔ found two 17th-century oil paintings\nthe paintings 还原为完整名称\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项把 his lost paintings 安在男子头上，可录音说的是他在垃圾堆里发现（found）画并\n上交，画不是他丢的、更不是他来打听自己丢的画，方向正好反了。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了录音 owners、rightful owner 里反复出现的 owner 一词，编出 \"他认识两幅失\n踪画的主人\"，可录音只说警方在找失主，从没说这位男子认识失主，是听到 owner 就想选\n的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：if the rightful owner is not found\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用录音 at a highway rest stop 这个真地点，却把动作篡改成 left（把画留在那\n里），而录音说的是他在那里 found（发现）画，地点是真的、动作是假的，典型原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：at a highway rest stop", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What is the art expert's conclusion about the two framed paintings?", "options": ["A) They are imitations.", "B) They are originals.", "C) They were stolen by an Italian boy.", "D) They came from the same artist."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are originals.\n【定位】An initial assessment by an art expert concluded the two framed paintings were originals,\npolice said.\n警方称，一位艺术专家的初步鉴定认为这两幅装框画作是真迹。\n【信号】结论动词 concluded 引出定性。录音里 An initial assessment by an art expert\nconcluded 用 concluded（得出结论）这个下结论的信号词，后面紧跟的 were originals\n就是专家的定性，正是题干要的答案。\n【替换】were originals ↔ They are originals.\n几乎原句保留\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 imitations（赝品）与专家结论 were originals（真迹）意思正好相反，是把定性极性\n反转的陷阱；A、B 一真一假对立，听准 originals 就该排除 A。\n借用原文：were originals\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用录音第 4 段 the Italian artist 的 Italian 和 a painting of a boy 的 boy，拼成 \"被\n一个意大利男孩偷走\"，可录音根本没提偷画，画里有个男孩、画家是意大利人都被偷换成\n了偷窃情节，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a painting of a boy by the 17th-century Dutch artist\nD) 与原文相反\n录音第 4 段明说一幅是意大利画家 Pietro Bellotti 所作、另一幅出自荷兰画家 Samuel van\nHoogstraten，是两位不同画家；选项却说出自 same artist，与原文事实直接矛盾。\n借用原文：One is a landscape painted by the Italian artist Pietro Bellotti dating to 1665. The other\nis a painting of a boy by the 17th-century Dutch artist Samuel van Hoogstraten", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What did the four-year-old girl attempt to do?", "options": ["A) Look after her grandfather.", "B) Leave the remote cold region.", "C) Save her sick grandmother.", "D) Flee from the threat of bears."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Save her sick grandmother.\n【定位】A four-year-old girl has walked eight kilometers through a snowy forest to seek help for her\nsick grandmother who later died of a heart attack.\n一个四岁女孩徒步八公里穿过白雪覆盖的森林，去为她生病的奶奶求助，奶奶后来死于心脏\n病发作。\n【信号】目的状语 to seek help for 直指意图。本句用不定式目的状语 to seek help for her sick\ngrandmother 点明女孩此行的目的——\"问意图\"的题，答案就藏在 to do 这个目的状语\n里，这是定位的信号。\n【替换】to seek help for her sick grandmother ↔ Save her sick grandmother.\n为奶奶求助=救奶奶（概括同义）\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段确实念到 her blind grandfather，A 项 Look after her grandfather. 就盗用这\n个\"爷爷\"原词制造\"听到就选\"的诱惑；但女孩求助的对象是奶奶不是爷爷，对象张冠李戴。\n借用原文：her blind grandfather\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 through a remote region in Siberia，B 项 Leave the remote cold region. 盗\n用 remote region 把女孩动机歪曲成\"为自己离开寒冷地区\"；实际她是为奶奶求助而穿越该\n地区，不是想离开它。\n借用原文：through a remote region in Siberia\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 braved the threat of bears，D 项 Flee from the threat of bears. 把这个醒目画\n面词搬进选项；但原文是说女孩\"冒着\"熊的威胁去求助，不是\"为逃避熊\"才出门，目的被偷\n换。\n借用原文：braved the threat of bears", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the girl's mother?", "options": ["A) She has to face a criminal charge.", "B) She was found lying motionless in the snow.", "C) She searched for her daughter in freezing cold.", "D) She works in childcare services."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She has to face a criminal charge.\n【定位】As a result, the girl's mother is facing a criminal case. She stands accused of leaving a minor\nin danger in the care of the elderly.\n结果，女孩的母亲正面临一桩刑事案件。她被指控把一个未成年人置于危险之中，交由老人\n照看。\n【信号】专门讲 mother 的那句直接应答题干。录音里唯一正面交代母亲的句子是 the girl's\nmother is facing a criminal case，紧接着用 stands accused of 补充指控内容——题干专\n问母亲，答案就锁定在这\"专讲母亲\"的两句上。\n【替换】is facing a criminal case ↔ She has to face a criminal charge.\n面临刑事案件=要面临刑事指控（近义词替换）\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音第 1 段说\"奶奶\"被发现时 motionless，B 项 She was found lying motionless in the\nsnow. 把这个画面安到\"母亲\"头上；motionless 是原词，但躺着不动的是奶奶不是妈妈。\n借用原文：waking up to find her grandmother motionless\nC) 未提及\n录音通篇讲严寒（negative 26 degrees Celsius 等），C 项 She searched for her\ndaughter in freezing cold. 借\"严寒\"主题编出\"母亲冒寒找女儿\"的情节；但原文从没说母亲\n外出寻找，是女孩自己出门求助。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 investigation from childcare services，D 项 She works in childcare services.\n盗用 childcare services 这个机构词；但原文是该机构要\"调查\"这件事，不是说母亲\"在那儿\n工作\"。\n借用原文：She also faces investigation from childcare services", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What happened to the little girl according to the news report?", "options": ["A) She was found in a forest after three days.", "B) She lay totally unconscious for three days.", "C) She suffered from the effects of severe cold.", "D) She was finally rescued by her relatives."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She suffered from the effects of severe cold.\n【定位】But though she was suffering from the effects of extreme cold, the child reportedly suffered\nno life-threatening effects.\n但尽管她正遭受极度寒冷带来的影响，据报道这个孩子并未出现危及生命的状况。\n【信号】末段交代结果，让步状语 though 引出真实状况。答案落在末段交代女孩结局的一句，信号\n是让步连词 though：though 引出的 suffering from the effects of extreme cold 正是女\n孩的真实遭遇，后半句\"无生命危险\"是补充，问遭遇就抓 though 这半句。\n【替换】suffering from the effects of extreme cold ↔ She suffered from the effects of severe cold.\nextreme cold=severe cold（近义形容词替换）\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音末句说\"去年一个三岁男孩\" survived alone for three days in a remote forest，A 项\nShe was found in a forest after three days. 盗用 forest 和 three days，把男孩的旧事安\n到这个女孩头上。\n借用原文：a three-year-old boy survived alone for three days\nB) 过度推断\nB 项 She lay totally unconscious for three days. 借男孩那句的 three days 拼出\"女孩昏\n迷三天\"；原文既没说女孩昏迷，也没说她经历三天，no life-threatening effects 反而说明\n她无大碍，程度被严重夸大。\n借用原文：the child reportedly suffered no life-threatening effects\nD) 与原文相反\n录音里女孩是\"独自外出为亲属求助\"，D 项 She was finally rescued by her relatives. 把方\n向反过来说成\"被亲属救出\"；relatives 算原文涉及的关系词，但谁救谁正相反。\n借用原文：to seek help for her sick grandmother", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say about her new friend Susan?", "options": ["A) She shows a real passion for taking photos.", "B) She has just returned from her hometown.", "C) She comes from the city of Cape Town.", "D) She has a truly amazing appearance."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She comes from the city of Cape Town.\n【定位】Her name is Susan and she's from South Africa.\n她叫 Susan，来自南非。\n【信号】女士介绍朋友的开场陈述。对话一开口，女士就主动交代 she's from South Africa，这\n是\"被介绍人的出身\"，正面回答了\"关于 Susan\"。随后第5段又点出家乡 It's called Cape\nTown.，两处合起来坐实她来自开普敦这座城市。\n【替换】she's from South Africa ↔ She comes from the city of Cape Town.\nfrom = comes from，国家细化到城市\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 She has very cool photos on her social media.——朋友圈有好照片，并没说\n她\"热爱拍照\"。选项把\"有酷照片\"偷换成\"对拍照有热情\"，是听到 photos 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：She has very cool photos on her social media.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 hometown 出现在 The photos of her hometown look amazing.，说的是\"家乡\n的照片好看\"，根本没说她\"刚从家乡回来\"。选项借 hometown 这个原词编了个时间动作。\n借用原文：The photos of her hometown look amazing.\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里 amazing 形容的是 The photos of her hometown look amazing.——家乡的照片\n惊艳，不是说\"她本人长相惊艳\"。选项把形容\"照片\"的词安到了\"她的外貌\"上。\n借用原文：The photos of her hometown look amazing.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say about Table Mountain?", "options": ["A) It is as famous as Cape Town.", "B) It has a flat surface at the top.", "C) It is green and free from pollution.", "D) It was named by European settlers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has a flat surface at the top.\n【定位】The mountain is called Table Mountain because it's flat at the top.\n这座山叫桌山，因为它顶部是平的。\n【信号】因果 because 后给出命名缘由即特征。The mountain is called Table Mountain\nbecause it's flat at the top. 中 because 后那句才是关键——它解释了山名的由来，也正\n面给出了这座山的物理特征\"顶部平坦\"。because 引出的正是答案落点。\n【替换】it's flat at the top ↔ It has a flat surface at the top.\nflat at the top = flat surface at the top\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 It's called Cape Town. 是城市名，但从没说桌山\"和开普敦一样有名\"。选项借\nCape Town 这个原词，凭空造了个\"知名度比较\"。\n借用原文：It's called Cape Town.\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 it's very green and windy——这是形容开普敦这座城市\"很绿、多风\"，既不是说桌\n山，也没说\"无污染\"。选项把 green 扩大成 free from pollution，程度被悄悄拔高。\n借用原文：it's very green and windy\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里 European settlers 出现在 The city was built by European settlers.——欧洲移\n民\"建了这座城\"，命名桌山的依据是\"顶平\"而非欧洲移民。选项把\"建城\"换成\"给山命名\"。\n借用原文：The city was built by European settlers.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do we learn from the conversation about the woman's friend Susan?", "options": ["A) She has British ancestors.", "B) She is of mixed blood.", "C) She grew up in India.", "D) She speaks several languages."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She has British ancestors.\n【定位】Susan is white. She says her ancestors were from Britain.\nSusan 是白人。她说她的祖先来自英国。\n【信号】明确指向 Susan 本人的直接陈述。第7段先讲南非\"很混杂\"，但话锋一转点名 Susan is\nwhite. She says her ancestors were from Britain.——主语明确是 Susan 本人，正面交代\n了她的血统来源，是答案句。前面那句\"很混杂\"是说整个国家，不是说她。\n【替换】her ancestors were from Britain ↔ She has British ancestors.\nancestors from Britain = British ancestors\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音 South Africa is very mixed 说的是\"南非（这个国家/人口）很混杂\"，紧接着却明说\nSusan is white.——她是白人，不是混血。选项把形容国家的 mixed 安到了她头上。\n借用原文：South Africa is very mixed\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音只说南非有 Indian people（印度裔居民），从没说 Susan\"在印度长大\"。选项借\nIndian 这个原词编了段她个人的成长史。\n借用原文：Indian people\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 Many languages are spoken in South Africa, but she only speaks English.——\n南非语言多，但她只会英语。选项说她\"会好几门语言\"，正好把 but 之后\"只说英语\"的真意\n说反了。\n借用原文：Many languages are spoken in South Africa, but she only speaks English.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman say about rugby in South Africa?", "options": ["A) It is an extremely violent sport.", "B) It is becoming a national sport.", "C) It is originated in New Zealand.", "D) It is more popular than football."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is more popular than football.\n【定位】I think they play football, but it's not as popular as rugby.\n我想他们踢足球，但足球不如橄榄球受欢迎。\n【信号】转折 but 后给出真正的流行度比较。I think they play football, but it's not as popular as\nrugby. 中 but 之后才是落点——\"足球不如橄榄球受欢迎\"，等于说橄榄球比足球更火。but\n后那句承载了答案，前半\"他们踢足球\"是铺垫。\n【替换】it's not as popular as rugby ↔ It is more popular than football.\nfootball not as popular as rugby = rugby more popular than football\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音说 the players push each other 且 I think it's very complicated.——只说球员互相\n推挤、规则复杂，从没说它\"极度暴力\"。选项借\"推挤/复杂\"的印象夸大成 extremely\nviolent。\n借用原文：the players push each other\nB) 未提及\n录音只说它在南非比足球受欢迎、在英国及前殖民地也流行，从没说它\"正在成为国球\"。\nnational sport 在原文里根本没出现，是无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 it's also popular in Britain and in other former British colonies like Australia and\nNew Zealand 说新西兰是\"它也流行\"的地方之一，并非\"起源地\"。选项把\"流行地\"偷换\n成\"起源于新西兰\"。\n借用原文：it's also popular in Britain and in other former British colonies like Australia and\nNew Zealand", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the woman advise the man to do?", "options": ["A) Prepare a study guide.", "B) Consult his advisors.", "C) Go over his notes regularly.", "D) Take stress-relief sessions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Prepare a study guide.\n【定位】Have you considered making a study guide? It's a tool you can make yourself to take the\nstress out of studying.\n你考虑过做一份学习指南吗？这是一个你可以自己制作的工具，能帮你把学习的压力卸\n下来。\n【信号】建议信号 Have you considered 引出的提议。女方用 Have you considered making a\nstudy guide? 这个建议句式回应男方的求助，「考虑过……吗」就是典型的提建议信号，信\n号后跟的 making a study guide 即为她建议的动作。\n【替换】Have you considered making a study guide? ↔ Prepare a study guide.\nmaking 改写成 Prepare，同义动词替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 Consult his advisors. 说的「咨询顾问」在整段对话里从未出现，女方建议的是自己做\n学习指南，不是去找顾问。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Go over his notes regularly. 借了对话里反复出现的 notes 来诱你，但女方此处的建\n议是做 study guide，并未叫他「定期复习笔记」；regularly（定期）更是录音里没有的。\n借用原文：my study folder is full of notes and worksheets\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Take stress-relief sessions. 抓住录音里 take the stress out of studying 的 stress 做\n文章，但录音说的是「学习指南能卸掉压力」，不是叫他去「上减压课程」；stress-relief\nsessions 是凭 stress 一词编出来的。\n借用原文：take the stress out of studying", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What is the biggest problem the man has with his studies?", "options": ["A) His worksheets are terribly messy.", "B) He finds the workload too heavy.", "C) His study folder is badly disorganized.", "D) He has difficulty taking notes quickly."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） His study folder is badly disorganized.\n【定位】My main problem is that my study folder is full of notes and worksheets, and is\nbadly disorganized.\n我主要的问题是，我的学习文件夹里塞满了笔记和练习题，而且乱得一塌糊涂。\n【信号】强调信号 My main problem is 直接点题。男方用 My main problem is 这个强调句式\n亲口点出最大困扰，main problem 就是直接指向答案的信号，其后描述的状况即为答案\n内容。\n【替换】my study folder is full of notes and worksheets, and is badly disorganized ↔\nHis study folder is badly disorganized.\nstudy folder 与 badly disorganized 原句几乎直取\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 His worksheets are terribly messy. 借了录音的 worksheets，但录音说乱的是整个\nstudy folder，worksheets 只是文件夹里装着的东西之一；把「乱」单独安到 worksheets\n头上是偷换对象。\n借用原文：my study folder is full of notes and worksheets\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 He finds the workload too heavy. 抓住男方开场 struggling with this semester's\nworkload 的 workload，但那只是他来求助的引子，他亲口说的 main problem 是文件夹\n乱，不是工作量大。\n借用原文：I'm really struggling with this semester's workload\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 He has difficulty taking notes quickly. 借录音里的 notes 做文章，但录音只说文件\n夹里塞满 notes 且很乱，从没提男方「记笔记速度慢」这个困难，taking notes quickly 是\n凭 notes 一词编造的。\n借用原文：my study folder is full of notes and worksheets", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What kind of learner does the woman say she is?", "options": ["A) A visual learner.", "B) An emotional learner.", "C) An organized learner.", "D) A logical learner."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） An emotional learner.\n【定位】I'm an emotional learner, which means I need to connect to information emotionally to\nunderstand it.\n我是一个情感型学习者，意思是我需要在情感上与信息建立联结才能理解它。\n【信号】第一人称自指 I'm an emotional learner。女方在介绍完 visual、logical 两类后，用 I'm\nan emotional learner 这个第一人称自指句点明自己的类别，I'm 就是「这是说我自己」的\n信号，与前面介绍他人类别区分开。\n【替换】I'm an emotional learner ↔ An emotional learner.\nemotional learner 原词对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 A visual learner. 借了录音里 visual learners 这一类，但视觉型对应的是男方（他说\nI'm very much dependent on vision），女方自称的是情感型；把男方的类别安给女方即\n张冠李戴。\n借用原文：visual learners prefer using images, pictures, colors, and maps\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 An organized learner. 蹭了全篇「整理笔记 organize」的氛围，但录音介绍的三类是\nvisual、logical、emotional，从没有 organized learner 这一类，更不是女方的自称。\n借用原文：reorganizing your notes using color-coded sections\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 A logical learner. 取了录音 Logical learners have a linear mind 这一类，但那是女\n方泛指介绍的一类人，她自称的是 emotional，不是 logical；安到女方头上属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Logical learners have a linear mind and would rather use logic, reasoning and systems", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman think the man can do with his notes?", "options": ["A) Arrange them using color and pictures.", "B) Restructure them in a logical way.", "C) Commit them to memory after class.", "D) Organize them into a well-connected story."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Arrange them using color and pictures.\n【定位】Well, I suggest reorganizing your notes using color-coded sections in your study guides, or\nusing idea mapping to lay out the information.\n嗯，我建议你用彩色分区的方式重新整理笔记放进学习指南里，或者用思维导图把信息铺陈\n出来。\n【信号】建议信号 I suggest 引出给男方的方法。女方用 I suggest reorganizing your notes\nusing color-coded sections 直接给男方建议，I suggest 是明确的建议信号，your notes\n表明是针对男方的笔记，信号后跟的「彩色分区、思维导图」即建议内容。\n【替换】using color-coded sections in your study guides, or using idea mapping ↔\nArrange them using color and pictures.\ncolor-coded、idea mapping 概括成 color and pictures\narrange my notes using color and pictures in place of text ↔\nArrange them using color and pictures.\n男方复述确认，几乎原句对应 A\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Restructure them in a logical way. 借了录音介绍 logical learners 时的 logic 一词，\n但女方给男方（视觉型）的建议是用颜色和图，不是「按逻辑重构」；logical 属另一类学\n习者的特征。\n借用原文：Logical learners have a linear mind and would rather use logic, reasoning and systems\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Commit them to memory after class. 蹭了录音 more quickly accessible 的「快」\n感，但录音说的是用颜色图让信息更快被取用，从没建议「课后把笔记背下来」；\nmemory、after class 都是录音没有的。\n借用原文：make it more quickly accessible\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 Organize them into a well-connected story. 取了录音 I organize my notes into a\nstory，但那是女方作为情感型学习者「自己」的做法，不是建议给视觉型的男方的；把女\n方的做法安成给男方的建议即张冠李戴。\n借用原文：As an emotional learner, I organize my notes into a story that I can connect to and recite\nto myself", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the golden rule?", "options": ["A) It is mainly based on a society's religion.", "B) It is interpreted differently in different times.", "C) It is a code of conduct based on laws and ethics.", "D) It is a moral principle to guide people's behavior."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a moral principle to guide people's behavior.\n【定位】The golden rule is a moral principle which states that you should treat others the way you\nwant to be treated yourself.\n黄金法则是一条道德准则，它主张你应当以你希望被别人对待的方式去对待别人。\n【信号】开篇“X is...”下定义句即落点。录音第一句就用 The golden rule is a moral principle\nwhich states that you should treat others the way you want to be treated yourself.\n直接给 golden rule 下定义，这种篇首“主题词 + is + 定义”的句子就是定义题的答案句信\n号，开篇即点题。\n【替换】a moral principle which states that you should treat others the way you want to be treated ↔\na moral principle to guide people's behavior\n“主张你该怎样待人”=“指导人的行为”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 the religion in their society，但只是说不同人接触到的“形式”受宗教等因素\n影响，从没说它“mainly based on religion”。A 项盗了 religion 这个原词，把“影响形式的\n因素之一”偷换成“主要建立在宗教上”，是听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：based on factors such as the religion in their society\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说 Different people tend to be exposed to different forms——是“不同人接触不同\n形式”，B 项 17B 式地改成“interpreted differently in different times”（不同时代有不同\n解读），把“不同人/不同形式”偷换为“不同时代”，且录音第 3 段强调 all forms... revolve\naround the same concept（内核其实相同），与 B 相悖。\n借用原文：Different people tend to be exposed to different forms of the golden rule\nC) 原词陷阱\na code of conduct based on laws and ethics 里的 laws and ethics 录音里确实反复出现\n（如 laws and ethical principles），但那是讲它与法律伦理的“冲突”关系，并非说 golden\nrule 本身“基于法律伦理”。录音定义它是 a moral principle（道德准则），C 把它的本质错\n挂到法律伦理上，是张冠李戴式的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：when it conflicts with laws and ethical principles", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is a notable criticism of the golden rule?", "options": ["A) It may lead to misunderstanding despite good intentions.", "B) It assumes that human beings are all good-natured.", "C) It may sometimes produce undesirable outcomes.", "D) It fails to consider the complexity of human relationships."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It may sometimes produce undesirable outcomes.\n【定位】A notable criticism of the golden rule is that its application can lead to undesirable outcomes,\nwhen it conflicts with laws and ethical principles.\n对黄金法则一条显著的批评是：当它与法律和伦理原则相冲突时，其应用可能导致不良\n后果。\n【信号】信号词 criticism 直接领出答案句。录音第 4 段以 A notable criticism of the golden rule\nis that... 开头，criticism 这个批评信号词一出，紧跟的 that 从句就是批评的具体内容——\n这正是答案句，靠信号词定位而非逐句扫读。\n【替换】its application can lead to undesirable outcomes ↔\nIt may sometimes produce undesirable outcomes.\nlead to=produce，原词 undesirable outcomes 几乎照搬\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt may lead to misunderstanding despite good intentions. 里的 misunderstanding\n（误解）和 good intentions（好意）录音通篇没提，批评句讲的是“与法律伦理冲突导致不\n良后果”，与误解无关，属凭常识想象的未提及项。\nB) 过度推断\nIt assumes that human beings are all good-natured. 是录音从未表达的论断——录音只\n说不同形式都 help you treat others better，并没说“它假设人人本性善良”。B 借\n“better/good”的善意语感引申出“假设人皆善良”，属过度推断。\n借用原文：they help you treat others better\nD) 未提及\nIt fails to consider the complexity of human relationships. 听着像个高级批评，但录音\n对它的批评只指向“与法律伦理冲突”，从未说它“没考虑人际关系的复杂性”。这是一个原文\n未提及、却被包装得很像批评的干扰项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the example of someone breaking the law serve to show?", "options": ["A) The golden rule is often in conflict with certain laws and ethical principles.", "B) The golden rule must sometimes give way to more important principles.", "C) Failure to follow the golden rule may lead to violation of laws and ethics.", "D) Observing the golden rule is the first step to becoming a responsible citizen."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The golden rule must sometimes give way to more important principles.\n【定位】Specifically, in the example described above, most individuals and societies choose to place\nlaws and ethical principles above the golden rule. This means that they strive to implement\nthe golden rule whenever possible, as long as it doesn't clash with a more important concept.\n具体来说，在上面描述的例子里，大多数个人与社会选择把法律和伦理原则置于黄金法则之\n上。这意味着，只要不与更重要的理念相冲突，他们就尽力践行黄金法则。\n【信号】例后“This means”总结句即论点落点。违法的例子在第 5 段，但作者在第 7 段用 most\nindividuals and societies choose to place laws and ethical principles above the\ngolden rule 与 This means that... 把例子升华成论断——“This means”这种总结信号之后\n才是例子真正要说明的观点。\n【替换】place laws and ethical principles above the golden rule ↔\nThe golden rule must sometimes give way to more important principles.\n“把法律置于其上”=“黄金法则让位于更重要原则”\nas long as it doesn't clash with a more important concept ↔ sometimes give way to\n“一旦与更重要理念冲突”=“有时须让位”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe golden rule is often in conflict with certain laws and ethical principles. 借了录音\n里的 conflict（conflicts with、clash）。但录音说的是“冲突时如何处理（让位）”，不是强\n调“它经常与法律冲突”；A 把例子的用意偷换成“频繁冲突”，且 often 程度过强，是原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：when it conflicts with laws and ethical principles\nC) 与原文相反\nFailure to follow the golden rule may lead to violation of laws and ethics. 用了\nlaws、violation 等近义词，但录音的逻辑是“遵守黄金法则反而可能让违法者逍遥（we\nshould let them go）”，即遵守它才可能冲突法律，而非“不遵守它就违法”。C 把因果方向\n整个弄反了。\n借用原文：the golden rule would suggest that we should let them go\nD) 过度推断\nObserving the golden rule is the first step to becoming a responsible citizen. 听着正\n能量，但录音从没说遵守它是“成为合格公民的第一步”，反而强调它要让位于更重要原则。\nD 借“principle、society”的语境拔高出一个原文没有的结论，属过度推断。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn from the current statistics about diversity in large corporations?", "options": ["A) Many of them find it rather difficult to manage.", "B) They have not seen as much diversity as desired.", "C) Many of them have an increasingly diversified staff.", "D) They have not quite grasped the concept of diversity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They have not seen as much diversity as desired.\n【定位】Obviously, the desire to bring about diversity has not translated into corporate reality.\n显然，想要实现多元化的愿望并没有变成公司里的现实。\n【信号】否定句 has not 是落点。这句的答案信号是含蓄否定 has not translated into corporate\nreality，前面 many large corporations stress the importance of diversity 是铺垫的美\n好愿望，转到 has not 才是真意——愿望没落地，这正是细节题要抓的结论句。\n【替换】the desire to bring about diversity has not translated into corporate reality ↔\nhave not seen as much diversity as desired\n愿望未落地=未达期望\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 Many of them find it rather difficult to manage. 听上去像盗用了后文 it might be\ndifficult to work with someone who has completely different views 的 difficult，但那\n是讲人们对多元化的顾虑，不是 current statistics 这道题问的统计现状，属答非所问。\n借用原文：it might be difficult to work with someone who has completely different views\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Many of them have an increasingly diversified staff. 盗用了 diversified 这个原\n词，方向却反了：录音明说 the typical manager in America still tends to be white and\nmale，统计显示多元化并未实现，C 却说员工日益多元，与原文相反。\n借用原文：the typical manager in America still tends to be white and male\nD) 未提及\n选项 They have not quite grasped the concept of diversity. 说公司没理解多元化的概\n念，但录音只说愿望没落实为现实，从没说公司不懂这个概念，属未提及、无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is the newly published study focused on?", "options": ["A) Initiatives to achieve diversity in large corporations.", "B) Advantages and disadvantages of a diversified team.", "C) People's attitudes towards diversity at the workplace.", "D) Innovative ideas and solutions resulting from diversity."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） People's attitudes towards diversity at the workplace.\n【定位】A team of researchers from the University of Basel published their new study about people's\nattitudes towards diversity at work.\n巴塞尔大学的一组研究者发表了他们关于人们对职场多元化态度的新研究。\n【信号】study about 直接点题。答案信号是介绍研究时的点题结构 published their new study\nabout people's attitudes towards diversity at work，about 后面紧跟的就是研究主题，\n这是主旨题最该锁定的总括句。\n【替换】people's attitudes towards diversity at work ↔\nPeople's attitudes towards diversity at the workplace.\nat work=at the workplace\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 Initiatives to achieve diversity in large corporations. 蹭了 diversity in large\ncorporations 这串原词，但 achieve diversity 的举措是第三、四段给组织的建议，不是这\n项研究本身聚焦的主题，把分论点冒充主旨，以偏概全。\n借用原文：These findings could help organizations become more diverse.\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Advantages and disadvantages of a diversified team. 对应录音第二段 On the\none hand / On the other hand 讲的多元化利弊，但那是人们态度的具体内容（人们一方\n面看到价值、一方面担心），属于主题之下的一个层面，拿来当全篇主旨则范围偏小，以偏\n概全。\n借用原文：On the one hand, many see value in diversity ... On the other hand, they assume that it\nmight be difficult\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Innovative ideas and solutions resulting from diversity. 盗用了 generate\ninnovative solutions 的原词，但那只是多元化的众多好处之一，是态度里看到价值的细\n节，远不是研究聚焦的总主题，属以偏概全。\n借用原文：encourage new ideas, and generate innovative solutions", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do the findings of the new study show?", "options": ["A) People prefer to work with team members similar to themselves.", "B) Employers attach great importance to their corporations' diversity.", "C) Employers differ from employees in their perspectives on diversity.", "D) Doubts about the practicability of diversity are gradually disappearing."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） People prefer to work with team members similar to themselves.\n【定位】In other words, when a person's own work group is involved, they tend to prefer team\nmembers who are similar to themselves.\n换句话说，当涉及一个人自己的工作团队时，他们往往倾向于选择和自己相似的团队成员。\n【信号】In other words 后是结论。答案信号是总结性标志 In other words，它后面紧跟的就是对\n前文的提炼结论 they tend to prefer team members who are similar to themselves，细\n节题正应锁定这句被点明的发现。\n【替换】they tend to prefer team members who are similar to themselves ↔\nPeople prefer to work with team members similar to themselves.\ntend to prefer=prefer to work with\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n选项 Employers attach great importance to their corporations' diversity. 蹭了 many\nlarge corporations stress the importance of diversity 的 importance、diversity，但那\n是开头讲公司在网站上的表态，不是这项研究的 findings，张冠李戴地把背景当成了发现。\n借用原文：many large corporations stress the importance of diversity on their websites\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 Employers differ from employees in their perspectives on diversity. 偷换了主\n体：录音区分的是 when a person's own work group is involved 与 when people make\ndecisions for others（自己是否被直接波及），而非 employers 和 employees 两种身\n份，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：when a person's own work group is involved ... But when people make decisions\nfor others\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 Doubts about the practicability of diversity are gradually disappearing. 盗用了\nDoubts about the practicability of diversity 整串原词，方向却反了：录音说当人被直接\n波及时这种疑虑 have a greater weight（分量更重），并非在逐渐消失，与原文相反。\n借用原文：Doubts about the practicability of diversity have a greater weight if a person is\ndirectly affected", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is a major key to success in international business according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Choosing the best time for signing a business contract.", "B) Changing one's form of communication from time to time.", "C) Laying equal stress on written and spoken communication.", "D) Using different forms of communication appropriately."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Using different forms of communication appropriately.\n【定位】Understanding when it's appropriate to exchange one form for another can be a major key to\nsuccess in international business.\n懂得在什么时候适合把一种沟通形式换成另一种，可以是国际商务成功的一个重要关键。\n【信号】题干原词 major key to success 在录音里原句重现。这句里 a major key to success in\ninternational business 与题干几乎逐字相同，是直接点题的下结论句，听到 key to\nsuccess 就该锁定它，再回头听它前半 Understanding when it's appropriate to\nexchange one form for another 才是答案的实质。\n【替换】when it's appropriate to exchange one form for another ↔\nUsing different forms of communication appropriately\n换一种形式=用不同形式，appropriate=appropriately\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nChoosing the best time for signing a business contract. 借了录音的商务背景和\nappropriate（合适）的影子，把它偷换成挑签合同的最佳时机，但录音说的是恰当地切换\n沟通形式，根本没讲签合同的时间点，属答非所问。\nB) 偷换概念\nChanging one's form of communication from time to time. 借用了换形式这个动作，却\n把录音强调的 when it's appropriate（看场合）偷换成 from time to time（时不时地）的\n随意更换，录音讲的是合适与否而非更换频率，属偷换概念。\nC) 与原文相反\nLaying equal stress on written and spoken communication. 借了书面与口头这对原文\n概念，却说要对两者等量看重，而录音通篇强调的是看场合选用合适的那种，不是一视同\n仁，方向正好相反。\n借用原文：Understanding when it's appropriate to exchange one form for another", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the passage say about spoken word agreements in some Middle East countries?", "options": ["A) They are regarded as seriously binding.", "B) They are seldom honored by business partners.", "C) They are taken as memos of understanding.", "D) They are to be confirmed in written form."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are regarded as seriously binding.\n【定位】In parts of the Middle East, you'll find spoken word agreements are seen as seriously binding.\n在中东部分地区，你会发现口头约定被看作具有严肃约束力。\n【信号】地名 Middle East 后紧跟对口头约定的定性。录音 In parts of the Middle East 一出现就\n锁定答案区，紧跟的 spoken word agreements are seen as seriously binding 正是对题\n干对象的直接定性，是顺着地名定位到的答案句。\n【替换】spoken word agreements are seen as seriously binding ↔ They are regarded as seriously binding\nseen as=regarded as，几乎原义对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThey are seldom honored by business partners. 与录音相反，录音说中东口头约定 are\nseen as seriously binding 且 A person's word is linked to their honor（一个人的话与\n荣誉相连，被高度看重），seldom honored（很少被信守）恰好反了过来。\n借用原文：A person's word is linked to their honor\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey are taken as memos of understanding. 盗用了录音原句 written contracts are\ntaken as memos of understanding，但录音里被当作谅解备忘录的是书面合同（written\ncontracts），不是题干问的口头约定，属张冠李戴——听到原词 memos of\nunderstanding 就选会中招。\n借用原文：written contracts are taken as memos of understanding\nD) 未提及\nThey are to be confirmed in written form. 借用了 written（书面）这个原文话题词，但\n录音对中东口头约定只说其约束力强，从未说它要以书面形式确认，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about the Western working culture?", "options": ["A) It has reached the highest level of evolution.", "B) It places a high value on written contracts.", "C) It regards written contracts as unalterable.", "D) It has seen a decline in verbal agreements."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It places a high value on written contracts.\n【定位】Western working culture tends to place a high value on the written word, and this reaches its\nhighest level of intensity when it comes to contracts.\n西方工作文化往往很看重书面文字，而这种看重在涉及合同时达到最强的程度。\n【信号】对象名 Western working culture 后紧跟其定性主干。录音 Western working culture\ntends to place a high value on the written word 一出现题干对象就锁定，紧跟的主\n干 place a high value on the written word 即对该文化的定性，是顺对象名定位到的答\n案句。\n【替换】place a high value on the written word ↔ It places a high value on written contracts\nthe written word 与 written contracts 同指书面，且原句下文点明 contracts\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt has reached the highest level of evolution. 盗用了录音 reaches its highest level of\nintensity 里的 highest level，却把 intensity（程度）换成 evolution（进化），还擅自拔\n高成进化最高级，录音从未说西方文化进化到顶，属过度推断+偷换原词。\n借用原文：this reaches its highest level of intensity\nC) 过度推断\nIt regards written contracts as unalterable. 把录音 something that must be strictly\ncarried out（必须严格执行）推到 unalterable（绝对不可更改），严格执行讲的是履行，\n不可更改讲的是能否修改，录音没说合同不能改，属过度推断。\n借用原文：written contracts tend to be seen as something that must be strictly carried out\nD) 未提及\nIt has seen a decline in verbal agreements. 借了 verbal/口头这个话题词，但录音讲西方\n文化只说它看重书面合同，从未提到口头协议出现下降，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How does a Japanese firm tend to view a written contract?", "options": ["A) Its details cannot be renegotiated.", "B) It has to be carried out to the letter.", "C) It strengthens business partnerships.", "D) Its terms may not be strictly binding."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Its terms may not be strictly binding.\n【定位】For example, a Japanese firm may have signed a contract, but they may not feel bound by\nevery detail of it—particularly if circumstances later change.\n举例来说，一家日本公司可能已经签了合同，但他们未必觉得受合同每一条细节的约束——\n尤其是当后来情况发生变化时。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真意，且 may not 软化约束。录音 may have signed a contract, but\nthey may not feel bound by every detail 里转折词 but 后面才是落点，may not feel\nbound（未必感到受约束）是日本公司对合同的真实态度，转折+may not 两个信号合起来\n指向弱约束。\n【替换】they may not feel bound by every detail of it ↔ Its terms may not be strictly binding\nnot feel bound 主动表述=not be binding 被动表述，detail≈terms\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nIts details cannot be renegotiated. 借了上文 renegotiate terms 的原词，但录音正说情\n况变化时这类公司可能想重新协商条款，并非不能重谈，cannot be renegotiated 与原意\n相反。\n借用原文：if your business partner wants to renegotiate terms that you thought were already\nagreed on\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt has to be carried out to the letter. 盗用了第6段说西方文化的 something that must\nbe strictly carried out，但严格照字面执行讲的是西方文化（美法德），不是题干问的日本\n公司，张冠李戴——听到 carried out 就选会中招。\n借用原文：written contracts tend to be seen as something that must be strictly carried out\nC) 与原文相反\nIt strengthens business partnerships. 与录音相反，录音说这种价值观差异 tend to\ncause many problems when it comes to business relationships（往往给商业关系带来\n很多问题），而非巩固伙伴关系，属与原文相反兼无中生有。\n借用原文：tend to cause many problems when it comes to business relationships", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) chew\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 could 后接动词原形\n【依据】把牙比作刀叉，自然就是\"咀嚼\"\n上文 You might think of your teeth as tools, like built-in knives and forks把牙比作刀\n叉，刀叉是用来切割食物、对应牙齿的功能正是咀嚼。\"如果牙不痛、能在任何情况下随便\n____\"——填 chew（咀嚼）最贴合牙齿\"工具\"的本职。\n【竞争词】E) continue 语义空泛、接不上刀叉的比喻\ncould just continue（只是继续）缺少明确对象，对不上前文把牙比作\"刀叉\"这个咀嚼工具的\n语境。\nM) swallow 义偏，刀叉不\"吞\"\nswallow 是\"吞咽\"，主语是喉咙而非牙齿；把牙比作刀叉强调的是咀嚼切割，不是吞咽，故\n不选。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) dental\n形容词\n【词性槽】物主代词 our 与名词 discomfort 之间作定语\n【依据】dental discomfort=牙齿带来的不适，扣牙痛主题\n全文讲牙痛，In spite of our dental discomfort——\"尽管我们有牙齿带来的不适\"，\ndental（牙齿的）修饰 discomfort 正好点出不适来自牙齿，与全文主题一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) defense\n名词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 mechanism 之间作定语的名词\n【依据】defense mechanism 固定说法 + 紧扣\"保护\"主旨\ndefense mechanism（防御机制）是地道固定搭配，且下文反复点明 So it's really a\nprotective mechanism more than anything else，\"防御=保护\"正好呼应。牙痛是一\n种\"防御机制\"，主旨契合度最高。\n【竞争词】I) emotional 义偏，与\"保护牙齿\"主旨冲突\nemotional mechanism（情感机制）说的是情绪，而本文讲的是牙痛\"保护牙齿\"的生理防御功\n能，与情感无关；下文 protective mechanism 也证明该填\"防御/保护\"类词，故排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) underneath\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 tissue 后作后置定语\n【依据】the tissue underneath=下面（深层）的组织，对应\"磨损露出内层\"\n句意是牙\"磨损到 ____ 的组织暴露出来\"。underneath 作后置定语表\"在下面的\"，the\ntissue underneath 即\"下层组织\"，牙被磨损后露出的正是深层组织，与第三段\"内层有神经\n会痛\"一致。\n【竞争词】H) downward 义偏\"向下运动\"，不表静态位置\ndownward 强调\"朝下的方向/运动\"（如 a downward trend），不用来表示\"位于下方的\"静态\n组织。the tissue downward 不通，故选表静态方位的 underneath。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— downward 与 underneath 都含\"下\"义，易选错。空 29\n要的是\"下面的组织\"，downward 也带\"向下\"且词性可作定语，迷惑性高；防坑：要的是静\n态\"位于下方\"用 underneath，downward 表运动方向，记住\"方向词≠方位词\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) continue\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 might 后接动词原形\n【依据】continue to do 搭配成立 + 逻辑通：会\"继续\"用受损的牙\n句意是\"如果牙不会痛，我们可能会 ____ 在伤牙的情况下用它们\"。continue to use（继续\n使用）搭配地道，逻辑也对——没有痛感的警示，人就会继续乱用牙导致受损，正好呼应主\n旨\"疼痛是保护机制\"。\n【竞争词】M) swallow 搭配不成立、语义不通\nswallow to use 搭配不存在（swallow 后不接 to do 表此意），且\"吞咽去使用牙齿\"语义荒\n谬，与上下文\"继续用受损的牙\"完全对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adult\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 teeth 前作定语的修饰语\n【依据】adult teeth=恒牙，对应\"换不了牙\"的难题\nadult teeth 指\"恒牙（成年人的牙）\"。紧接下句 unlike crocodiles, we can't replace\nthem——恒牙坏了换不了，所以\"damaging adult teeth is a problem\"，逻辑完整。\nadult 在这里取\"成年的、恒久的\"义，正好与\"不可替换\"对应。\n【竞争词】G) dental 语义冗余：dental 本就是\"牙的\"，修饰 teeth 重复\ndental 意为\"牙齿的\"，dental teeth（牙齿的牙齿）语义重复、不通；况且 dental 已是空 27\n\"dental discomfort\"的答案，按\"用过即划\"也不能再用。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) replace\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can't 后必跟动词原形\n【依据】对比鳄鱼\"换牙\"，人类不能\"替换\"牙齿\n上文说牙受损是个问题，紧接 unlike crocodiles, we can't __(32)__ them：鳄鱼牙掉了能\n长新的，而人\"不能 ____它们\"。能与\"鳄鱼能、人不能\"这层对比对上的只有 replace（替换\n牙齿），逻辑正好解释了为什么\"damaging adult teeth is a problem\"。\n【竞争词】C) chew 与上下文逻辑相反\nwe can't chew them（不能咀嚼牙齿）说不通，牙是用来咀嚼食物的，不是被咀嚼的对象，且\n与\"鳄鱼\"的对比毫无关系。\nE) continue 搭配与语义都不成立\nwe can't continue them 意思含糊（无法继续它们？），既接不上 them=牙齿这个宾语的逻\n辑，也对不上\"鳄鱼能换牙\"的对比。\nM) swallow 义不对，与对比脱节\nswallow them（吞下牙齿）与\"damaging adult teeth is a problem\"的因果完全无关，纯属\n误填。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) contains\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语，需第三人称单数动词\n【依据】形态唯一 + 语义吻合：内层\"含有\"血管和神经\nthat layer of the tooth __(33)__ both blood vessels and nerves——牙的内层\"含有\"血管\n和神经，contains（含有）与宾语 blood vessels and nerves 搭配自然，且为单三形式，\n主谓一致，是唯一答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) triggered\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 pain 后作后置定语的过去分词\n【依据】triggered by=由……引发，与\"冷热\"作诱因搭配\npain triggered by heat or cold——\"由冷热引发的疼痛\"。triggered by 表\"被……触发/引\n发\"，heat or cold 是疼痛的直接诱因，搭配精准、语义通顺。\n【竞争词】B) associated associated with 而非 associated by\nassociated 的固定搭配是 associated with（与……相关），不和 by 连用。这里空后是 by heat\nor cold，搭配不合；况且 associated 更适合空 35 的 associated with，此处该选 triggered。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— associated 与 triggered 都表\"相关/引发\"，极易混填。空\n34 pain __(34)__ by heat or cold里，associated（相关的）词性也对、词义也沾\"与冷热\n有关\"，看着也通；防坑：associated 只搭 with、不搭 by，空后是 by 就必须选\ntriggered，把这对词的固定介词背死。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adult", "B) associated", "C) chew", "D) contains", "E) continue", "F) defense", "G) dental", "H) downward", "I) emotional", "J) implies", "K) mammal", "L) replace", "M) swallow", "N) triggered", "O) underneath"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) associated\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 + 空 + with，需过去分词作表语\n【依据】be associated with=与……相关联，固定搭配 + 余下唯一候选\nbe associated with 是地道固定搭配，表\"与……相关\"。they feel pain, which may be\nassociated with, say, drinking something very cold——这种痛\"可能与喝很凉的东西相\n关\"，语义通顺，且空后正是 with，搭配完全吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Clues about the culture of a company can be found on its website.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】公司官网（website）\n【定位】Start with the most obvious place first: the company's website.\n先从最显眼的地方入手：公司官网。\n【改写】Clues about the culture of a company can be found on its website ↔\nStart with the most obvious place first: the company's website\n建议去某处找 = 那里能找到\n【辨邻】 I An industry review website like Glassdoor 里的 website 被 industry review 修\n饰，所指的是行业评价站，而 its website 中的 its 回指\"a company\"，指的是公司自\n家官网，两者不是同一对象。\nAn industry review website like Glassdoor is another spot to check", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It can be difficult to know the real situation in a company until you become part of it.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】成为其中一员之前难以了解真实情况\n【定位】However, figuring out what a company is like before you actually work there can be a bit of\na challenge.\n然而，在你真正去那里工作之前，要弄清一家公司到底是什么样，会有点难。\n【改写】It can be difficult to know the real situation in a company ↔\nfiguring out what a company is like ... can be a bit of a challenge\ndifficult = a challenge\nuntil you become part of it ↔ before you actually work there\n正反对调的同义时间限定\n【辨邻】 C Company culture can have a big impact on how you feel about your work 只说\n文化影响感受，没有把\"入职前看不清\"这一困境点出来，属于话题相邻而陈述不同。\nCompany culture can have a big impact on how you feel about your work, so you want to make\nsure you sign an offer letter with an organization you're truly excited about", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is impossible for a job applicant to have every expectation met.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不可能样样期待都被满足\n【定位】What are my chances of finding all of those things with one employer? Slim to none—believe\nme, I've looked. ... Unfortunately, this is reality, you can't have everything you want but a few\nare certainly achievable.\n在同一个雇主身上把那些（理想清单）全找到的概率有多大？微乎其微——相信我，我找\n过。……不幸的是，这就是现实，你不可能样样都满足，但其中一些确实可以达成。\n【改写】It is impossible for a job applicant to have every expectation met ↔\nWhat are my chances of finding all of those things with one employer? Slim to none\nSlim to none = impossible\nhave every expectation met ↔ you can't have everything you want\ncan't + everything 是 every…not 的同义结构\n【辨邻】 L My boss would genuinely listen to and value all of my ideas and suggestions 等\n句都是虚拟语气描述理想图景，是 M 段判断的对象，但 L 段自己没说\"这不可能\"，所\n以不是 Q38 的源头段。\nMy boss would genuinely listen to and value all of my ideas and suggestions. My co-workers would\nall be friendly with one another", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Simply by reading its description, the author found the job offered ideal.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】只看描述就觉得理想\n【定位】On paper, the job seemed perfect for me: The position was completely in line with my degree,\nthe duties and responsibilities were compatible with my interests, and the office maintained a\nwell-stocked kitchen that would satisfy my every snack desire.\n纸面上看，这份工作对我来说近乎完美：岗位完全契合我的专业，职责与我的兴趣相符，办\n公室还有一个装得满满当当的零食厨房，能满足我所有的零嘴欲望。\n【改写】Simply by reading its description ↔ On paper\nOn paper = 仅看纸面描述\nthe author found the job offered ideal ↔ the job seemed perfect for me\nideal ≈ perfect\n【辨邻】 B There was only one small problem: I simply didn't get along with the company\nculture 已经在讲\"实际不合\"，是对 A 段的反转，本身不再承担\"作者觉得这份工作理\n想\"这一陈述。\nThere was only one small problem: I simply didn't get along with the company culture", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Job applicants are advised to make a written list of their likes and dislikes in their previous employment.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把以前工作里喜恶写成清单\n【定位】Start by writing down the things you didn't like about previous employers, as well as the parts\nyou really valued.\n先动笔，把以前的雇主里你不喜欢的地方、以及你真正看重的部分都写下来。\n【改写】make a written list ↔ writing down\n写下 = 列书面清单\nlikes and dislikes in their previous employment ↔\nthe things you didn't like about previous employers, as well as the parts you really valued\n抽象名词 ↔ 关系从句展开\n【辨邻】 E it's important to have a solid handle on what exactly you want from your\nemployer 只讲\"心里要有数\"，没有把\"动笔写下\"这一可操作动作点出来，所以不是\nQ40 的源头段。\nMany of us have an easier time identifying the things that we absolutely don't want", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "At the end of an interview, a job applicant should seize the opportunity to get answers to their urgent questions.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】面试末尾抓住提问机会\n【定位】You know that part at the end of a job interview when the hiring manager asks if you\nhave any questions, and you just stare across the table blankly with your mouth hanging\nopen? That's the perfect opportunity to speak up and get your burning company culture\nquestions answered!\n你知道面试结尾招聘经理问\"还有什么问题吗\"、而你只能隔着桌子张着嘴干瞪眼的那段时间\n吗？那正是开口发声、解开你那些急迫文化问题的好时机！\n【改写】At the end of an interview ↔ that part at the end of a job interview\n几乎逐字复现\nseize the opportunity to get answers to their urgent questions ↔\nThat's the perfect opportunity to speak up and get your burning company culture questions\nanswered\nurgent = burning（比喻义）", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "To begin with, job applicants should be clear what they expect from their future employer.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】先弄清对未来雇主的期待\n【定位】First of all, know what you want. ... So before you can determine whether you and a specific\ncompany would be compatible together, it's important to have a solid handle on what exactly\nyou want from your employer.\n首先，要清楚自己想要什么。……所以在你判断自己与某家具体的公司是否合得来之前，先\n把自己到底想从雇主那里得到什么搞清楚，这一点很重要。\n【改写】To begin with, job applicants should be clear what they expect from their future employer ↔\nbefore you can determine whether you and a specific company would be compatible together,\nit's important to have a solid handle on what exactly you want from your employer\nhave a solid handle on = be clear about\nTo begin with ↔ First of all\n首句连接词同义替换\n【辨邻】 F Start by writing down the things you didn't like about previous employers 明确\n有\"写下来\"动作，而 Q42 只要求\"be clear\"——心里清楚，并未要求\"写\"，所以不是\nQ42 的源头段。\nStart by writing down the things you didn't like about previous employers, as well as the parts you\nreally valued", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Job applicants should read with a critical eye what is written about a company on the website.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对网站上对公司的评价持批判眼光\n【定位】An industry review website like Glassdoor is another spot to check in order to find some\ninsider information about what you can expect about a company. However, remember to take\nthe reviews you read with a grain of salt—many of them are written by scorned employees.\n像 Glassdoor 这种行业评价网站，是另一个可以去查一查公司内幕的地方，能让你知道对\n一家公司可以期待什么。但要记住，对你读到的评价要保留一点怀疑——其中不少是被亏待\n过的员工写的。\n【改写】read with a critical eye what is written about a company on the website ↔\nremember to take the reviews you read with a grain of salt—many of them are written by\nscorned(被鄙视的) employees\nwith a grain of salt = critical eye\n【辨邻】 G Read through their copy and blog 是建议直接读、判断语气风格，没有 take with a\ngrain of salt 这种保留态度，因此不承担 Q43 的陈述。\nRead through their copy and blog. Do they use formal, direct language? Or is it casual,\nconversational, and maybe even a little humorous?\nH Are they sharing photos of their team's Thursday afternoon barbeque or\nHalloween costume contest? 是判断氛围的依据，不涉及\"对所读内容保持怀疑\"。\nAre they sharing photos of their team's Thursday afternoon barbeque or Halloween\ncostume contest?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Job satisfaction has a lot to do with company culture.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】工作满意度与公司文化挂钩\n【定位】Company culture can have a big impact on how you feel about your work, so you want to\nmake sure you sign an offer letter with an organization you're truly excited about.\n公司文化会对你对工作的感受产生很大影响，所以你要确保自己签下 offer 的那家公司是你\n真心向往的。\n【改写】Job satisfaction ↔ how you feel about your work\n名词 ↔ how 从句\nhas a lot to do with company culture ↔\nCompany culture can have a big impact on how you feel about your work\nhave a lot to do with = have a big impact on\n【辨邻】 B I simply didn't get along with the company culture 是个案陈述（\"我\"和这家公司\n文化合不来），没有把\"文化 ↔ 满意度\"提升到普遍道理，所以承担陈述的是把规律点\n透的 C 段，而不是 B 段。\nThey favored a more rigid, closed-door, corporate atmosphere, while I would have preferred\nsomething more collaborative and open\nN you're still trying to determine whether or not the company is a good fit for\nyou 强调的是\"判断是否合适\"，并未涉及\"对工作的感受/满意度\"这一陈述。\nyou should also look at the process through a similar lens", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A chat with an insider of a company can give job applicants very useful information when they prepare for an interview.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】与公司内部人员聊有助于面试准备\n【定位】Send a quick message to a current or previous company employee on LinkedIn or by email\nand ask if they would be willing to have a quick conversation with you about the organization\nin general. If you get a yes to your request for a chat, you'll be armed with some pretty\npowerful and helpful information heading into your interview!\n在 LinkedIn 上或通过邮件给这家公司的现任或前任员工发一条短消息，问他们是否愿意和\n你简短聊聊关于这家公司的事情。如果对方答应了你的聊天请求，你就会带着相当强大、相\n当有用的信息走进面试现场！\n【改写】A chat with an insider of a company ↔\nSend a quick message to a current or previous company employee on LinkedIn or by email\nand ask if they would be willing to have a quick conversation with you\ninsider ↔ current or previous company employee；chat ↔ quick conversation\ncan give job applicants very useful information when they prepare for an interview ↔\nyou'll be armed with some pretty powerful and helpful information heading into your\ninterview\nvery useful ↔ pretty powerful and helpful；prepare for ↔ heading into\n【辨邻】 K that part at the end of a job interview when the hiring manager asks if you\nhave any questions 已经在\"面试当中\"了，与 prepare for an interview 的\"面试\n前\"时间节点不一致。\nThat's the perfect opportunity to speak up and get your burning company culture\nquestions answered!", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about students enrolled in online classes?", "options": ["A) They can access course materials easily.", "B) They are unmotivated to learn.", "C) They can learn at their own pace.", "D) They rarely fulfil the course requirements."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about students enrolled in online classes? 在问「就上网课\n的学生这件事，作者具体说了什么」，是对单一陈述的事实核对。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the author say about X，标准的 say about 双宾结构，关于 X 的\n对象是 students enrolled in online classes，其中 enrolled in online classes 是过去分\n词短语作 students 的后置定语，等于 students who are enrolled in online classes，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】First of all, students lack the desire to learn, and they simply complete their assignments to\nreceive credit for a passing grade rather than genuinely engage with the course material.\n首先，学生缺乏学习的欲望，他们只是完成作业以拿到及格分数，而不是真正与课程内容打\n交道。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴：宣传方观点≠作者观点\nThey can access course materials easily. 取自 P1 对网课的宣传性描述，并非作者本\n人就「学生」给出的评价，问的是作者对学生说了什么，不是对网课说了什么。\nB) ✓ 正确\nlack the desire to learn ↔ unmotivated to learn\n预判要找「学生没有学习动力」的表达，四个选项里只有 They are unmotivated to\nlearn. 直接对应 lack the desire to learn，故锁定。\nC) 张冠李戴：照抄被驳斥的卖点\nThey can learn at their own pace. 原句正是 learn the material at their own pace 的\n近乎照抄，是作者列为反驳对象的卖点，不是作者对学生的论断。\nD) 与原文相反：rarely 类否定词反转事实\nThey rarely fulfil the course requirements. 说学生很少完成课程要求，但 they\nsimply complete their assignments to receive credit for a passing grade 明示他们\n恰恰会完成作业、只是为拿学分，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think of online course assignments?", "options": ["A) They are made convenient to mark.", "B) They are meant to facilitate interaction.", "C) They are based on easily accessible material.", "D) They are given to accommodate students' needs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of online course assignments? 指向作者对某一具体事物的\n判断句，仍属细节核对。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author think of X，think of 在此意为「认为…如何」，相当于 What\nis the author’s opinion of X，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】They are not designed for students to interact with the material in depth but designed to be\ngraded easily to accommodate such a large number of students.\n这些作业的设计不是为了让学生深入与材料互动，而是为了便于打分，以应对如此庞大的学\n生群体。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndesigned to be graded easily ↔ made convenient to mark\n预判锁定「便于打分」的语义，仅 They are made convenient to mark. 直接对应；其\n他选项要么扯到互动，要么扯到材料/学生需求，不在 designed to 的目的范围。\nB) 与原文相反：not…but… 的前半句反着用\nThey are meant to facilitate interaction. 把作业说成「促进互动」，但 not designed\nfor students to interact with the material in depth 明确否定了「互动」目的。\nC) 无中生有：制造原文未提及的属性\nThey are based on easily accessible material. 说作业基于「容易获取的材料」，原文\nP4 没有任何句子谈论材料的获取难易，凭空捏造。\nD) 偷换概念：动词宾语被替换\nThey are given to accommodate students' needs. 把 accommodate 的对象从原文\nto accommodate such a large number of students 中的「大量学生（人数）」偷换\n成「学生的需求」，目的指向被改。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say is one disadvantage of online classes?", "options": ["A) They are frequently scheduled at irregular times.", "B) They make professors' offices much less accessible.", "C) They tend to increase professors' burden of responding to students' emails.", "D) They provide little chance for students to build relationships with each other"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is one disadvantage of online classes? 中 one\ndisadvantage 是单数，明示只问其中一个具体缺点，是定点取信息的细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author say is one disadvantage of online classes，say 后是\n省略 that 的宾语从句 (that) one disadvantage of online classes is what，what 提前\n到句首形成疑问，需注意「what 在从句里实为表语」这一点，否则容易把 say 误读成不\n及物。\n【定位】There is also little interaction amongst students. It can be harder for students to create study\ngroups and form relationships with their peers.\n学生之间的互动也很少。要想组建学习小组、和同伴建立关系会更难。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念 + 与原文相反\nThey are frequently scheduled at irregular times. 说直播课「频繁地」被安排在不规\n律时间，原文 Live sessions are infrequent 恰恰说直播课很少，且原文谈的是「时间冲\n突」而非「时间不规律」。\nB) 偷换概念：时间不便 ≠ 空间不可达\nThey make professors' offices much less accessible. 把焦点放在「办公室更不可\n达」，但 The office hours of the professor may also be during inconvenient times\n只说「办公时间不便」，没有说办公室不可达，accessible 与 convenient times 不是\n同义。\nC) 无中生有：从一个事实派生未提及的后果\nThey tend to increase professors' burden of responding to students' emails. 谈\n教授回邮件「负担加重」，原文 Most interaction with the professor has to be\nthrough email which is often impersonal 只说邮件互动「冷冰冰」，并未提教授\n负担。\nD) ✓ 正确\nform relationships with their peers ↔ students to build relationships with each other\nIt can be harder for students to create study groups ↔ provide little chance\n预判核心是「学生 + 学生之间难建立关系」，They provide little chance for students\nto build relationships with each other 在主体、动作、方向上全部命中。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What problem may arise if classes go online?", "options": ["A) More students may find it easy to be absent from them.", "B) Teachers will worry about poor internet connections.", "C) Some students may have difficulty attending them.", "D) Schools with limited resources will be at a disadvantage."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What problem may arise if classes go online? 问「在网课条件下会出现什么问题」，问\n的是文中已写明的某一具体后果，不需要推论。\n【拆句】主干 What problem may arise，后接 if 条件状语从句 if classes go online，arise 为不及\n物动词「出现」，结构清晰；注意 go online 这个搭配指「转为线上」，不要望文生义译作\n「上网」。\n【定位】Not all students have access to these types of resources, whether it is for financial or other\nreasons, and some students can be put at a disadvantage.\n并非所有学生都能用上这些资源，无论是出于经济还是其他原因，有些学生因此可能处于不\n利地位。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：把后果嫁接到主观行为\nMore students may find it easy to be absent from them. 谈学生「更容易缺席」，\n原文从未谈学生主观逃课难易，凭空添加。\nB) 张冠李戴：替换受影响主体\nTeachers will worry about poor internet connections. 把网络问题的担忧者换成\nteachers，原文 P7 通篇是 students 受影响，无任何关于教师担忧的描述。\nC) ✓ 正确\nNot all students have access to these types of resources ↔\nSome students may have difficulty attending them\nsome students can be put at a disadvantage ↔ may have difficulty\n预判核心「部分学生上不了网课」，选项 Some students may have difficulty\nattending them 主体、动作、可能性三要素与原文一一对位。\nD) 张冠李戴：把人替换成机构\nSchools with limited resources will be at a disadvantage. 把 at a disadvantage\n的主体换成 schools，但 some students can be put at a disadvantage 明确主语\n是 students。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think constitutes a key part of genuine education?", "options": ["A) Acquisition of useful knowledge.", "B) Training of real-life skills on campus.", "C) Development of students' personalities.", "D) Cultivation of analytical thinking ability."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think constitutes a key part of genuine education? 仍是问作者\n明示的具体内容，文末已直接点出，无需推断。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author think X constitutes Y，think 后为宾语从句，从句里\nWhat 同时是 constitutes 的主语，constitutes = 构成；该结构需要把 What 还原到从句\n主语位才能读顺，是四级长难句的小考点。\n【定位】However, they fail to provide a genuine education with an emphasis on convenience rather\nthan critical thinking.\n然而，网课未能提供真正的教育，因为它强调便利而非批判性思维。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全：用常识替代作者主张\nAcquisition of useful knowledge. 把「真教育」化约为「获取有用知识」，但 P8 强调\n的是思维而非知识，且原文也未把「有用知识」列为真教育的关键。\nB) 无中生有：用文章主题的相邻概念造干扰\nTraining of real-life skills on campus. 强调「校园里」「真实生活技能」两个限定，但\nP8 既未谈 real-life skills 也未谈 on campus，是利用「网课—线下」对立常识捏造的\n对立项。\nC) 无中生有：搬用流行教育口号\nDevelopment of students' personalities. 把真教育的关键说成「学生人格的发展」，\n原文 P8 通篇未涉及 personalities/character 之类的人格概念。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncritical thinking ↔ analytical thinking ability\n预判核心是 critical thinking，四个选项里只有 Cultivation of analytical thinking\nability. 直接对应 critical thinking 的同义改写，故锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think open debate can do?", "options": ["A) Help the public to better understand science.", "B) Clear up confusion in the scientific community.", "C) Settle disputes between universities and government agencies.", "D) Prevent information from being used incorrectly by the public."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think open debate can do是一个具体的做什么问题，不含infer、\nimply、mainly about等推断或主旨信号词，属于围绕一个话题点的细节定位题\n【拆句】主干是What does the author think open debate can do，think后省略that，引出宾语\n从句open debate can do (what)。问句的What其实是宾语从句里do的宾语，被前置到句\n首。结构简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】If this trend spurs scientists to agree more quickly about the best solutions to our problems—\nand at the same time helps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly\n—then this is good for everyone, including scientists.\n如果这一趋势能推动科学家更快地就我们面临问题的最佳解决方案达成一致，并同时帮助公\n众更清楚地观察科学讨论的过程，那么对所有人，包括科学家在内，都是好事。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhelps the public observe the process of scientific discourse more clearly ↔\nHelp the public to better understand science\n预判让公众更清楚地观察、理解科学讨论与Help the public to better understand\nscience在语义上同构，仅是observe到understand、scientific discourse到science的\n上下位替换\nB) 偷换概念\nClear up confusion in the scientific community说的是消除科学界内部的困惑，但原\n文potential for information to be used incorrectly, leading to public confusion点\n明confusion存在于公众一侧、而非科学界内部\nC) 无中生有\nSettle disputes between universities and government agencies声称开放辩论能解\n决高校与政府机构之间的纠纷；原文虽提及halls of universities and government\nagencies，但只是说辩论的发生地，并未说要去settle什么disputes\nD) 与原文相反\nPrevent information from being used incorrectly by the public把防止信息被误用说\n成open debate的功能；但原文there's potential for information to be used\nincorrectly, leading to public confusion里，这一可能性是开放辩论的副作用而非它的\n解决目标", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why did a recent debate published in The New York Times arouse concerns among experts?", "options": ["A) It might hinder the progress in medical research.", "B) It might breed public distrust in modern medicine.", "C) It might add to the difficulty of getting research funds.", "D) It might prevent medical scientists reaching consensus."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why did a recent debate published in The New York Times arouse concerns among\nexperts问的是某一具体事件下专家担心的具体原因，原因可在文中直接找到，不需要跨段\n推断\n【拆句】主干是Why加did加a recent debate加arouse加concerns。a recent debate后带过去分词\n短语published in The New York Times作后置定语，限定是哪一场debate；among\nexperts限定concerns的范围。理清后题干即那场登在某报上的辩论，为何让专家担心。\n【定位】But some experts worried that such public speculation might lead people to believe that\ndisagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus over the safety\nand efficiency of modern-day medicine.\n但一些专家担心，这种公开揣测可能让人们相信：对细节的分歧意味着科学界对现代医药的\n安全与效率缺乏足够的共识。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt might hinder the progress in medical research谈的是阻碍医学研究的进展，原文\n只说公开辩论可能让公众产生误解，并未说会拖慢医学研究本身\nB) ✓ 正确\nmight lead people to believe that disagreement about the details meant a lack of adequate\nscientific consensus over the safety and efficiency of modern-day medicine\n↔ It might breed public distrust in modern medicine\n预判中的对现代医药产生不信任正好对应public distrust in modern medicine；原文的\n多步表述被压缩成一个名词distrust，是典型的名词化同义替换\nC) 无中生有\nIt might add to the difficulty of getting research funds说会增加获得研究经费的难\n度；原文从未提及funds、funding、money相关概念\nD) 偷换概念\nIt might prevent medical scientists reaching consensus说会阻止医学科学家达成共\n识；原文是meant a lack of adequate scientific consensus——即让公众以为科学界\n没共识，但事实是科学界已有共识，并不是公开辩论会真的让科学家无法达成共识", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why does the author say some experts' anxiety seems misplaced?", "options": ["A) Debating scientific issues at a conference is now old-fashioned.", "B) Diverse topics can be debated by both scientists and the public.", "C) Debates about science are accessible to the public anyway.", "D) Scientists can voice their opinions whatever way they like."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author say some experts' anxiety seems misplaced问的是作者下此判断\n的依据，依据明示在原文紧随其后的句子中，属于因果细节\n【拆句】Why does the author say后接一个宾语从句some experts' anxiety seems misplaced。\nanxiety前是some experts'所有格作定语；seems是系动词、misplaced是表语形容词。理\n解为为什么作者说某些专家的担心是放错地方的。\n【定位】Gone are the days of going to a conference and debating scientific issues, and that's good\nbecause those gatherings were not diverse enough and excluded many important voices.\nThese days, the public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place.\n去开会、在会上辩论科学问题的日子已经过去了，这是好事，因为那些聚会不够多元，把许\n多重要的声音排除在外。如今，无论辩论在哪里举行，公众都能接触到。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nDebating scientific issues at a conference is now old-fashioned只是原文Gone are\nthe days of going to a conference的复述，确实存在于文中，但它只是支撑句之一，\n不是作者用来回答为什么misplaced的核心理由；核心理由是公众如今随处可获取辩论\nB) 偷换概念\nDiverse topics can be debated by both scientists and the public说的是多元话题可\n被科学家与公众共同辩论；原文diverse是修饰those gatherings本身（聚会不够多\n元），并不是说话题多元，更没说公众能与科学家共同辩论\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe public can access debates about science regardless of where they take place ↔\nDebates about science are accessible to the public anyway\n预判中的无论在哪都能接触到正是accessible to the public anyway的内涵；从access\n（动词）到accessible（形容词）的词性转换加主被动互换是四级最典型的同义替换手法\nD) 张冠李戴\nScientists can voice their opinions whatever way they like说的是科学家可以以他们\n喜欢的方式发声；原文谈论的是公众能接触到辩论，主语和动作都不是科学家", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author suggest scientists do about public debate?", "options": ["A) Have more discussions about it.", "B) Embrace it with open arms.", "C) Formulate new rules for it.", "D) Restrain it to a rational degree."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest scientists do about public debate问的是一个具体建\n议，答案在原文祈使句中能直接找到，无需推断\n【拆句】主干What does the author suggest scientists do。suggest后接的是宾语从句省略\nthat，scientists是从句主语、do是从句动词（虚拟语气省略should），about public\ndebate是范围状语。句意：作者建议科学家们对于公共辩论应该怎么做。\n【定位】But rather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous,\nwherever they are held.\n但与其回避这类对话，不如让辩论变得透明而有力，无论它们在哪里举行。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nHave more discussions about it说对公共辩论本身展开更多讨论——它把公共辩论变\n成被讨论的对象；原文是要科学家参与到公共辩论中，而不是开会讨论公共辩论应不应\n该做\nB) ✓ 正确\nrather than avoiding such conversations, let the debates be transparent and vigorous ↔\nEmbrace it with open arms\n预判拥抱并积极参与与Embrace it with open arms在意象与态度极性上完全一致；\nrather than avoiding的反面正是embrace\nC) 与原文相反\nFormulate new rules for it说要为公共辩论制定新规则；原文it may feel like a place\nwith few restraints or rules承认公共辩论缺规则，但下一句紧接着以But rather than\navoiding such conversations转向拥抱而非立规则，作者并未建议立新规\nD) 与原文相反\nRestrain it to a rational degree说要把公共辩论约束到一个合理程度；原文却用let the\ndebates be transparent and vigorous强调放开而非约束，方向相反", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author say about science in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) It is transmitted through textbooks.", "B) It is what proves valid and true to life.", "C) It is a dynamic and self-improving process.", "D) It is a collection of facts and established rules."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about science in the last paragraph问的是末段里关于\nscience的具体描述，不问全文主旨也不问态度极性，定位段已给出，属段内细节题\n【拆句】主干What does the author say about science。in the last paragraph是地点状语限定回\n答范围。say about science是动词搭配对…作出说明。理解为在末段里作者关于science说\n了什么。\n【定位】If the public is to understand that science is an honorably self-correcting process, the idea that\nscience is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed.\n如果想让公众明白科学是一个光荣的自我修正过程，那么科学就是教科书里一组固定事实这\n一观念就必须被抛弃。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is transmitted through textbooks说科学通过教科书传播；原文the idea that\nscience is a fixed set of facts in a textbook needs to be dismissed恰好是作者要否\n定的观念\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt is what proves valid and true to life说科学是被证明有效且符合现实的东西；原文\nthere's a need for scientific debates to be perceived as open and true to life里\ntrue to life修饰的是scientific debates（辩论）如何被感知，而不是用来定义science\n本身\nC) ✓ 正确\nscience is an honorably self-correcting process ↔ It is a dynamic and self-improving process\n预判自我修正的动态过程与a dynamic and self-improving process逐字对应；self-\ncorrecting对应self-improving是同根同义的复合形容词替换\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is a collection of facts and established rules说科学是事实与既定规则的集合——\n这与原文science is a fixed set of facts in a textbook想抛弃的旧观念完全对齐", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
